{
  "dom_id": "step-q134284",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14233881/assets/278c910d3ce96e613e04cb1b849e57775064c49de45972358c07bfa90d882c8b.png)\n\nThe residual plot above was constructed after fitting a linear regression model on a set of bivariate numerical data $(x,y).$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. When $x=2,$ the regression equation overestimates the value of $y.$\n\nII. The residuals appear to be randomly scattered about the horizontal axis (there are no obvious trends).\n\nIII. This linear regression is probably a good fit for the data.",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The residual equals the actual value minus the estimated value:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the plot, the residual at $x=2$ is positive. So, we have that $\\text{Actual}>\\text{Estimated},$ and therefore the model underestimates (*not* overestimates) this data point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The plot shows that the residuals appear randomly scattered about the horizontal axis (there are no obvious trends).\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the residuals are close to $0$ and are randomly scattered above and below the horizontal axis, we can conclude that our regression line is probably a good fit for the given data.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
